        "name": "Joe Hisaishi - A Very Busy Kiki (from the Kiki's Delivery Service Soundtrack)",
        "description": "I, too, have been a very busy Kiki. I've been in the process of moving across the country for the past couple months, and haven't been able to play Beat Saber for about a month now. It's been quite an eventful and stressful time in my life, but I wanted to put something short and lighthearted out for everyone, and for myself as well. Hopefully the moving truck with my PC setup (and everything else) comes in the next few days.\n\nJoe Hisaishi is a legendary Japanese composer, primarily known for his work on numerous Studio Ghibli films. This brief track hails from one of the earlier and more well-known ones, \"Kiki's Delivery Service\" from 1989. The plot follows a young witch through a coming-of-age scenario where she moves out on her own to get a job, and ends up delivering for a bakery. It's a very sweet and wholesome slice-of-life anime film; I highly recommend watching it if you haven't already.\n\nFirst time using the Triangle environment, and I thought it would fit nicely with the triple time waltz feel of the piece. The color scheme for the notes comes from Kiki's outfit (her dark robe and iconic red bow). The colors for the walls and lights come from the album cover, which features a picturesque rural town landscape. I wanted this map to have the player sway back and forth, to sort of mirror how Kiki felt when riding her broomstick. The green walls sort of represent the trees that Kiki would fly in between, which I tried to make especially prominent in the last third of the map.\n\nThough this was meant to be a short and stressful project, I had some great input from folks whose mapping work I admire that heavily feature sway, namely @AllPoland and @GojiCrafter . Big thanks to @SynthVenom, @ben208, and @Nebelmonster for testplaying. Also, thanks to some more folks from the Beat Saber Mapping Discord server who provided additional feedback during the mapping process: @Undeceiver, @Serephor, @vivry, @minsiii (bonus thanks for deconstructing the issues with my overall mapping philosophy lol), @ob1cb, and @Poochy . If I missed you I'm sorry, there was a lot to scroll through! <3",
